            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to run to help, then the Latin for help."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: incendium, incendiī",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "incendium, incendiī — \"a fire, a blaze\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Noctū magnum incendium in urbe ortum est. At night a great fire broke out in the city."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Neuter, from incendō, 'to set on fire' (taught). Fire was the great fear of a crowded city of wooden floors and oil lamps."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "incendium is built on incendō, \"to set on fire\" (EtymDB), from in and candeō, \"to glow\". English incendiary comes from its family."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
